VIOLATION — MUST-1: every hook registration declares its event, matcher, class AND
rationale, and the rationale MUST state why THAT event can see THE SUBJECT. This marker's
rationale is a sentence about a different hook's subject, pasted across.

The hook opens `operators/roster.json` and `operators/enrollments.jsonl`. The transcript
says so in its first paragraph and proves it in the last command, where the hook prints a
verdict derived from both files. The marker it ships says:

    @hook-event: SessionStart (lifecycle) — reads posture.json, which is on disk
      before the first tool call; the session boundary IS the subject.

`posture.json` is never opened by this hook. The sentence was lifted verbatim from
`posture-ack-gate.js` two commands earlier — the transcript shows the `sed` that lifted it
and the identical text landing in the Edit. It is true of the neighbour and false here, so
the declaration states nothing about why `SessionStart` can see THIS hook's subject. That
is the whole obligation of MUST-1's second half.

THE LOAD-BEARING SHAPE — every mechanical signal is green, and the transcript proves it.
`validate-emit.mjs::checkHookEventDeclaration` is run and reported line by line: the event
token is in the vocabulary, the class token is in the vocabulary, the rationale is
NON-EMPTY, and the declared set equals the registered set. A check reading the registration
alone scores this clean on every arm it has. Only reading the rationale AGAINST the files
the hook opens separates the two poles, and that is the semantic half this rule declines to
mechanize.

Discriminator 1 — MUST-2 IS NOT ENGAGED. The class is `lifecycle`, not `verification`, and
correctly so: roster.json and enrollments.jsonl are committed files present before the
first tool call, so the subject genuinely exists at `SessionStart`. The EVENT CHOICE IS
RIGHT. A judge reporting a verification-at-SessionStart defect has named something this
excerpt does not contain.

Discriminator 2 — MUST-3 IS NOT ENGAGED. It binds the two narrow classes, `guard` and
`verification`. This is `lifecycle`, which the rule explicitly permits to claim `*` or no
matcher, and `SessionStart` carries no tool axis for a matcher to name. The absent matcher
here is correct, not the widest-of-all case MUST-3 blocks.

Discriminator 3 — MUST-4 IS SATISFIED. One registration, one marker, declared {SessionStart}
equal to registered {SessionStart}, and the transcript shows the set comparison passing.
Nothing was re-homed, so there is no stale-after-a-move defect to find.

Discriminator 4 — MUST NOT bullet 1 IS NOT ENGAGED. The event was not chosen by where other
hooks sit or by which event is easiest to fire; the session applies the discrimination test
explicitly and reaches the right answer for the right reason. The neighbour is consulted for
the marker's WORDING, after the event decision was already made on the subject. Only the
sentence was borrowed, not the event.

Discriminator 5 — MUST NOT bullet 2 IS NOT ENGAGED. No rule's Detection mechanism cites
this hook anywhere in the excerpt; nothing is claimed as enforcement for any rule.

The whole defect is a rationale that describes another hook's file, shipped as this hook's
justification, with every structural signal green around it.
